# Java Clean Code

## Principles

- **Explicit over implicit**: Clear naming, no magic numbers, obvious intent
- **Single responsibility**: One class/method = one reason to change
- **Fail fast**: Validate early, throw meaningful exceptions
- **No dead code**: Remove commented blocks, unused imports, unreachable code

## Naming

```java
// Classes/Records: PascalCase, noun
public class CsvReader {}
public record CampaignAggregate(String campaignId, long totalImpressions) {}

// Methods: camelCase, verb
public void parseRow(String line) {}
public Optional<CampaignAggregate> findTopByCtr(int limit) {}

// Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
private static final int CTR_DECIMAL_PLACES = 4;
private static final int CPA_DECIMAL_PLACES = 2;
```

## Method Design

- Max ~20–30 lines per method; extract helpers when longer
- Max 3–4 parameters; use record/DTO for more
- Early returns to reduce nesting
- One level of abstraction per method

```java
// BAD: nested, mixed abstraction
public void process() {
    for (var row : rows) {
        if (row != null) {
            var parts = row.split(",");
            if (parts.length >= 6) {
                var campaignId = parts[0].trim();
                // ... 20 more lines
            }
        }
    }
}

// GOOD: extracted, clear
public void process() {
    for (var row : rows) {
        parseAndAggregate(row).ifPresent(this::addToAggregate);
    }
}
```

## Null & Optional

- Use `Optional` for return types that may be absent
- Avoid `Optional` as field or parameter
- Prefer `Objects.requireNonNull` for required params

## Error Messages

- English, descriptive, include context
- Example: `"Invalid CSV row: expected 6 columns, got %d: %s"`

## Code Smells to Avoid

| Smell | Fix |
|-------|-----|
| Magic numbers | Named constants |
| Long parameter list | Record/DTO |
| Deep nesting | Early return, extract method |
| Silent catch | Log and rethrow or handle |
| God class | Split into focused classes |
